<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    USB host intent-filter: lets Android offer "always open this app for this
    device" when a board is attached. Without a matching entry the operator has
    to grant USB permission by hand after every replug — and on a pinned kiosk
    screen that also means unpinning first, which is not something to ask of a
    technician on site.

    Matched by VENDOR ONLY, on purpose. product-id is optional in this schema,
    and leaving it out makes each line cover every bridge that vendor ships —
    so a board with a new chip works the day it arrives instead of after an
    APK release. This file used to name CH340G's exact pair, and the
    micromarket relay board (a CH340K: same vendor, different product) fell
    straight through it.

    These four vendors make effectively all the USB-to-UART bridges we meet:

      0x1A86 = 6790   QinHeng   CH340 / CH341 / CH9102
      0x0403 = 1027   FTDI      FT232 and friends
      0x10C4 = 4292   Silicon Labs  CP210x
      0x067B = 1659   Prolific  PL2303

    Same list as BoardClient.knownUsbSerialVids and MainActivity's
    usbSerialVendors; keep the three in step.
-->
<resources>
    <usb-device vendor-id="6790" />
    <usb-device vendor-id="1027" />
    <usb-device vendor-id="4292" />
    <usb-device vendor-id="1659" />
</resources>
